msgid ""
"班超字仲升，扶风平陵人，徐令彪之少子也。为人有大志，不修细节。然内孝谨，居家常执勤苦，不耻劳辱。有口辩，而涉猎书传。永平五年，兄固被召诣校书郎，超与母随至洛阳。家贫，常为官佣书以供养。久劳苦，尝辍业投笔叹曰：“大丈夫无它志略，犹当效傅介子、张骞立功异域，以取封侯，安能久事笔研闲乎？”左右皆笑之。超曰：“小子安知壮士志哉！”其后行诣相者，曰：“祭酒，布衣诸生耳，而当封侯万里之外。”超问其状。相者指曰：“生燕颔虎颈，飞而食肉，此万里侯相也。”久之，显宗问固“卿弟安在”，固对“为官写书，受直以养老母”。帝乃除超为兰台令史，后坐事免官。\n"
"\n"
"十六年，奉车都尉窦固出击匈奴，以超为假司马，将兵别击伊吾，战于蒲类海，多斩首虏而还。固以为能，遣与从事郭恂俱使西域。\n"
"\n"
"超到鄯善，鄯善王广奉超礼敬甚备，后忽更踈懈。超谓其官属曰：“宁觉广礼意薄乎？此必有北虏使来，狐疑未知所从故也。明者睹未萌，况已著邪。”乃召侍胡诈之曰：“匈奴使来数日，今安在乎？”侍胡惶恐，具服其状。超乃闭侍胡，悉会其吏士三十六人，与共饮，酒酣，因激怒之曰：“卿曹与我俱在绝域，欲立大功，以求富贵。今虏使到裁数日，而王广礼敬即废；如令鄯善収吾属送匈奴，骸骨长为豺狼食矣。为之柰何？”官属皆曰：“今在危亡之地，死生从司马。”超曰：“不入虎穴，不得虎子。当今之计，独有因夜以火攻虏，使彼不知我多少，必大震怖，可殄尽也。灭此虏，则鄯善破胆，功成事立矣。”众曰：“当与从事议之。”超怒曰：“吉凶决于今日。从事文俗吏，闻此必恐而谋泄，死无所名，非壮士也！”众曰：“善”。初夜，遂将吏士往奔虏营。会天大风，超令十人持鼓藏虏舍后，约曰：“见火然，皆当鸣鼓大呼。”馀人悉持兵弩夹门而伏。超乃顺风纵火，前后鼓噪。虏众惊乱，超手格杀三人，吏兵斩其使及从士三十馀级，馀众百许人悉烧死。明日乃还告郭恂，恂大惊，既而色动。超知其意，举手曰：“掾虽不行，班超何心独擅之乎？”恂乃恱。超于是召鄯善王广，以虏使首示之，一国震怖。超晓告抚慰，遂纳子为质。还奏于窦固，固大喜，具上超功效，并求更选使使西域。帝壮超节，诏固曰：“吏如班超，何故不遣而更选乎？今以超为军司马，令遂前功。”超复受使，固欲益其兵，超曰：“愿将本所从三十馀人足矣。如有不虞，多益为累。”\n"
"\n"
"是时于窴王广德新攻破莎车，遂雄张南道，而匈奴遣使监护其国。超既西，先至于窴。广德礼意甚踈。且其俗信巫。巫言：“神怒何故欲向汉？汉使有䯄马，急求取以祠我。”广德乃遣使就超请马。超密知其状，报许之，而令巫自来取马。有顷，巫至，超即斩其首以送广德，因辞让之。广德素闻超在鄯善诛灭虏使，大惶恐，即攻杀匈奴使者而降超。超重赐其王以下，因镇抚焉。\n"
"\n"
"时龟兹王建为匈奴所立，倚恃虏威，据有北道，攻破疏勒，杀其王，而立龟兹人兜题为疏勒王。明年春，超从闲道至疏勒。去兜题所居槃橐城九十里，逆遣吏田虑先往降之。敕虑曰：“兜题本非疏勒种，国人必不用命。若不即降，便可执之。”虑既到，兜题见虑轻弱，殊无降意。虑因其无备，遂前劫缚兜题。左右出其不意，皆惊惧奔走。虑驰报超，超即赴之，悉召疏勒将吏，说以龟兹无道之状，因立其故王兄子忠为王，国人大恱。忠及官属皆请杀兜题，超不听，欲示以威信，释而遣之。疏勒由是与龟兹结怨。\n"
"\n"
"十八年，帝崩。焉耆以中国大丧，遂攻没都护陈睦。超孤立无援，而龟兹、姑墨数发兵攻疏勒。超守盘橐城，与忠为首尾，士吏单少，拒守岁馀。肃宗初即位，以陈睦新没，恐超单危不能自立，下诏征超。超发还，疏勒举国忧恐。其都尉黎弇曰：“汉使弃我，我必复为龟兹所灭耳。诚不忍见汉使去。”因以刀自刭。超还至于窴，王侯以下皆号泣曰：“依汉使如父母，诚不可去。”互抱超马脚，不得行。超恐于窴终不听其东，又欲遂本志，乃更还疏勒。疏勒两城自超去后，复降龟兹，而与尉头连兵。超捕斩反者，击破尉头，杀六百馀人，疏勒复安。\n"
"\n"
"建初三年，超率疏勒、康居、于窴、拘弥兵一万人攻姑墨石城，破之，斩首七百级。超欲因此叵平诸国，乃上疏请兵。曰：“臣窃见先帝欲开西域，故北击匈奴，西使外国，鄯善、于窴即时向化。今拘弥、莎车、疏勒、月氏、乌孙、康居复愿归附，欲共并力破灭龟兹，平通汉道。若得龟兹，则西域未服者百分之一耳。臣伏自惟念，卒伍小吏，实愿从谷吉效命绝域，庶几张骞弃身旷野。昔魏绛列国大夫，尚能和辑诸戎，况臣奉大汉之威，而无鈆刀一割之用乎？前世议者皆曰取三十六国，号为断匈奴右臂。今西域诸国，自日之所入，莫不向化，大小欣欣，贡奉不绝，唯焉耆、龟兹独未服从。臣前与官属三十六人奉使绝域，备遭艰厄。自孤守疏勒，于今五载，胡夷情数，臣颇识之。问其城郭小大，皆言‘倚汉与依天等’。以是效之，则葱领可通，葱领通则龟兹可伐。今宜拜龟兹侍子白霸为其国王，以步骑数百送之，与诸国连兵，岁月之间，龟兹可禽。以夷狄攻夷狄，计之善者也。臣见莎车、疏勒田地肥广，草牧饶衍，不比敦煌、鄯善间也，兵可不费中国而粮食自足。且姑墨、温宿二王，特为龟兹所置，既非其种，更相厌苦，其埶必有降反。若二国来降，则龟兹自破。愿下臣章，参考行事。诚有万分，死复何恨。臣超区区，特蒙神灵，窃兾未便僵仆，目见西域平定，陛下举万年之觞，荐勋祖庙，布大喜于天下。”书奏，帝知其功可成，议欲给兵。平陵人徐干素与超同志，上疏愿奋身佐超。五年，遂以干为假司马，将㢮刑及义从千人就超。\n"
"\n"
"先是莎车以为汉兵不出，遂降于龟兹，而疏勒都尉番辰亦复反叛。会徐干适至，超遂与干击番辰，大破之，斩首千馀级，多获生口。超既破番辰，欲进攻龟兹。以乌孙兵彊，宜因其力，乃上言：“乌孙大国，控弦十万，故武帝妻以公主，至孝宣皇帝，卒得其用。今可遣使招慰，与共合力。”帝纳之。八年，拜超为将兵长史，假鼓吹幢麾。以徐干为军司马，别遣卫候李邑护送乌孙使者，赐大小昆弥以下锦帛。\n"
"\n"
"李邑始到于窴，而值龟兹攻疏勒，恐惧不敢前，因上书陈西域之功不可成，又盛毁超拥爱妻，抱爱子，安乐外国，无内顾心。超闻之，叹曰：“身非曾参而有三至之谗，恐见疑于当时矣。”遂去其妻。帝知超忠，乃切责邑曰：“纵超拥爱妻，抱爱子，思归之士千馀人，何能尽与超同心乎？”令邑诣超受节度。诏超：“若邑任在外者，便留与从事。”超即遣邑将乌孙侍子还京师。徐干谓超曰：“邑前亲毁君，欲败西域，今何不缘诏书留之，更遣它吏送侍子乎？”超曰：“是何言之陋也！以邑毁超，故今遣之。内省不疚，何恤人言！快意留之，非忠臣也。”\n"
"\n"
"明年，复遣假司马和恭等四人将兵八百诣超，超因发疏勒、于窴兵击莎车。莎车阴通使疏勒王忠，啖以重利忠遂反从之，西保乌即城。超乃更立其府丞成大为疏勒王，悉发其不反者以攻忠。积半岁，而康居遣精兵救之，超不能下。是时月氏新与康居婚，相亲，超乃使使多赍锦帛遗月氏王，令晓示康居王，康居王乃罢兵，执忠以归其国，乌即城遂降于超。\n"
"\n"
"后三年，忠说康居王借兵，还据损中，密与龟兹谋，遣使诈降于超。超内知其奸而外伪许之。忠大喜，即从轻骑诣超。超密勒兵待之，为供张设乐。酒行，乃叱吏缚忠斩之。因击破其众，杀七百馀人，南道于是遂通。\n"
"\n"
"明年，超发于窴诸国兵二万五千人，复击莎车。而龟兹王遣左将军发温宿、姑墨、尉头合五万人救之。超召将校及于窴王议曰：“今兵少不敌，其计莫若各散去。于窴从是而东，长史亦于此西归，可须夜鼓声而发。”阴缓所得生口。龟兹王闻之大喜，自以万骑于西界遮超，温宿王将八千骑于东界徼于窴。超知二虏已出，密召诸部勒兵，鸡鸣驰赴莎车营，胡大惊乱奔走，追斩五千馀级，大获其马畜财物。莎车遂降，龟兹等因各退散，自是威震西域。\n"
"\n"
"初，月氏尝助汉击车师有功，是岁贡奉珍宝、符拔、师子，因求汉公主。超拒还其使，由是怨恨。永元二年，月氏遣其副王谢将兵七万攻超。超众少，皆大恐。超譬军士曰：“月氏兵虽多，然数千里逾葱领来，非有运输，何足忧邪？但当收谷坚守，彼饥穷自降，不过数十日决矣。”谢遂前攻超，不下，又钞掠无所得。超度其粮将尽，必从龟兹求救，乃遣兵数百于东界要之。谢果遣骑赍金银珠玉以赂龟兹。超伏兵遮击，尽杀之，持其使首以示谢。谢大惊，即遣使请罪，愿得生归。超纵遣之。月氏由是大震，岁奉贡献。\n"
"\n"
"明年，龟兹、姑墨、温宿皆降，乃以超为都护，徐干为长史。拜白霸为龟兹王，遣司马姚光送之。超与光共胁龟兹废其王尤利多而立白霸，使光将尤利多还诣京师。超居龟兹它乾城，徐干屯疏勒。西域唯焉耆、危须、尉犂以前没都护，怀二心，其馀悉定。\n"
"\n"
"六年秋，超遂发龟兹、鄯善等八国兵合七万人，及吏士贾客千四百人讨焉耆。兵到尉犂界，而遣晓说焉耆、尉犂、危须曰：“都护来者，欲镇抚三国。即欲改过向善，宜遣大人来迎，当赏赐王侯已下，事毕即还。今赐王彩五百匹。”焉耆王广遣其左将北鞬支奉牛酒迎超。超诘鞬支曰：“汝虽匈奴侍子，而今秉国之权。都护自来，王不以时迎，皆汝罪也。”或谓超可便杀之。超曰：“非汝所及。此人权重于王，今未入其国而杀之，遂令自疑，设备守险，岂得到其城下哉！”于是赐而遣之。广乃与大人迎超于尉犂，奉献珍物。\n"
"\n"
"焉耆国有苇桥之险，广乃绝桥，不欲令汉军入国。超更从它道厉度。七月晦，到焉耆，去城二十里，营大泽中。广出不意，大恐，乃欲悉驱其人共入山保。焉耆左侯元孟先尝质京师，密遣使以事告超，超即斩之，示不信用。乃期大会诸国王，因扬声当重加赏赐，于是焉耆王广、尉犂王汎及北鞬支等三十人相率诣超。其国相腹久等十七人惧诛，皆亡入海，而危须王亦不至。坐定，超怒诘广曰：“危须王何故不到？腹久等所缘逃亡？”遂叱吏士收广、汎等于陈睦故城，斩之，传首京师。因纵兵钞掠，斩首五千馀级，获生口万五千人，马畜牛羊三十馀万头，更立元孟为焉耆王。超留焉耆半岁，慰抚之。于是西域五十馀国悉皆纳质内属焉。\n"
"\n"
"明年，下诏曰：“往者匈奴独擅西域，寇盗河西，永平之末，城门昼闭。先帝深愍边萌婴罗寇害，乃命将帅击右地，破白山，临蒲类，取车师，城郭诸国震慑响应，遂开西域，置都护。而焉耆王舜、舜子忠独谋悖逆，恃其险隘，覆没都护，并及吏士。先帝重元元之命，惮兵役之兴，故使军司马班超安集于窴以西。超遂逾葱领，迄县度，出入二十二年，莫不賔从。改立其王，而绥其人。不动中国，不烦戎士，得远夷之和，同异俗之心，而致天诛，蠲宿耻，以报将士之仇。《司马法》曰：‘赏不逾月，欲人速睹为善之利也。’其封超为定远侯，邑千户。”\n"
"\n"
"超自以久在绝域，年老思土。十二年，上疏曰：“臣闻太公封齐，五世葬周，狐死首丘，代马依风。夫周齐同在中土千里之闲，况于远处绝域，小臣能无依风首丘之思哉？蛮夷之俗，畏壮侮老。臣超犬马齿歼，常恐年衰，奄忽僵仆，孤魂弃捐。昔苏武留匈奴中尚十九年，今臣幸得奉节带金银护西域，如自以寿终屯部，诚无所恨，然恐后世或名臣为没西域。臣不敢望到酒泉郡，但愿生入玉门关。臣老病衰困，冒死瞽言，谨遣子勇随献物入塞。及臣生在，令勇目见中土。”而超妹同郡曹寿妻昭亦上书请超曰：\n"
"\n"
"妾同产兄西域都护定远侯超，幸得以微功特蒙重赏，爵列通侯，位二千石。天恩殊绝，诚非小臣所当被蒙。超之始出，志捐躯命，兾立微功，以自陈效。会陈睦之变，道路隔绝，超以一身转侧绝域，晓譬诸国，因其兵众，每有攻战，辄为先登，身被金夷，不避死亡。赖蒙陛下神灵，且得延命沙漠，至今积三十年。骨肉生离，不复相识。所与相随时人士众，皆已物故。超年最长，今且七十。衰老被病，头发无黑，两手不仁，耳目不聦明，扶杖乃能行。虽欲竭尽其力，以报塞天恩，迫于岁暮，犬马齿索。蛮夷之性，悖逆侮老，而超旦暮入地，久不见代，恐开奸宄之源，生逆乱之心。而卿大夫咸怀一切，莫肯远虑。如有卒暴，超之气力不能从心，便为上损国家累世之功，下弃忠臣竭力之用，诚可痛也。故超万里归诚，自陈苦急，延颈逾望，三年于今，未蒙省录。\n"
"\n"
"妾窃闻古者十五受兵，六十还之，亦有休息不任职也。缘陛下以至孝理天下，得万国之欢心，不遗小国之臣，况超得备侯伯之位，故敢触死为超求哀，匄超馀年。一得生还，复见阙庭，使国永无劳远之虑，西域无仓卒之忧，超得长蒙文王葬骨之恩，子方哀老之惠。诗云：“民亦劳止，汔可小康，惠此中国，以绥四方。”超有书与妾生诀，恐不复相见。妾诚伤超以壮年竭忠孝于沙漠，疲老则便捐死于旷野，诚可哀怜。如不蒙救护，超后有一旦之变，兾幸超家得蒙赵母、卫姬先请之贷。妾愚戆不知大义，触犯忌讳。\n"
"\n"
"书奏，帝感其言，乃征超还。\n"
"\n"
"超在西域三十一岁。十四年八月至洛阳，拜为射声校尉。超素有匈胁疾，既至，病遂加。帝遣中黄门问疾，赐医药。其年九月卒，年七十一。朝廷愍惜焉，使者吊祭，赠赗甚厚。子雄嗣。\n"
"\n"
"初，超被征，以戊己校尉任尚为都护。与超交代。尚谓超曰：“君侯在外国三十馀年，而小人猥承君后，任重虑浅，宜有以诲之。”超曰：“年老失智，任君数当大位，岂班超所能及哉！必不得已，愿进愚言。塞外吏士本非孝子顺孙，皆以罪过徙补边屯。而蛮夷怀鸟兽之心，难养易败。今君性严急，水清无大鱼，察政不得下和。宜荡佚简易，宽小过，緫大纲而已。”超去后，尚私谓所亲曰：“我以班君当有竒策，今所言平平耳。”尚至数年，而西域反乱，以罪被征，如超所戒。\n"
"\n"
"有三子。长子雄，累迁屯骑校尉。会叛羌寇三辅，诏雄将五营兵屯长安，就拜京兆尹。雄卒，子始嗣，尚清河孝王女阴城公主。主顺帝之姑，贵骄淫乱，与嬖人居帷中，而召始入，使伏床下。始积怒，永建五年，遂拔刃杀主。帝大怒，腰斩始，同产皆弃巿。超少子勇。"
msgstr ""
"Ban Chao, whose courtesy name was Zhongsheng, was a man of Pingling in Fufeng and the youngest son of Ban Biao, prefect of Xu. As a man he had great ambitions and did not trouble himself with small proprieties, yet at home he was filial and careful, and he took on hard work without ever being ashamed of toil or humiliation. He was a ready speaker and had read widely in the books and commentaries. In the fifth year of Yongping, his elder brother Ban Gu was summoned to the post of collator of texts, and Chao accompanied their mother to Luoyang. The family was poor, and he often hired himself out to copy documents for the government in order to support them. After long hardship he once broke off his work, threw down his brush, and sighed: \"A great man who has no other plan should still model himself on Fu Jiezi and Zhang Qian, win merit in foreign lands, and so obtain a marquisate. How can he spend his life among brush and inkstone?\" Those around him all laughed at him. Chao said: \"How should you little fellows know the ambition of a true man?\" Later he went to a physiognomist, who said: \"Libationer, you are merely a common scholar in plain cloth, yet you shall be enfeoffed as a marquis ten thousand li away.\" Chao asked him on what signs he based this. The physiognomist pointed and said: \"You have a swallow's jaw and a tiger's neck; you will fly and eat flesh. This is the look of a marquis of ten thousand li.\" After a long while, Emperor Ming asked Ban Gu, \"Where is your younger brother?\" Gu replied: \"He is copying documents for the government and takes his pay to support our old mother.\" The emperor then appointed Chao clerk of the Orchid Terrace; afterwards he was dismissed from office over some matter.\n"
"\n"
"In the sixteenth year, Dou Gu, colonel of chariots and cavalry, went out against the Xiongnu. He made Chao acting major and sent him with troops to strike Yiwu by a separate route. Chao fought at Lake Pulei, took many heads and captives, and returned. Gu judged him capable and sent him together with the aide Guo Xun as envoy to the Western Regions.\n"
"\n"
"When Chao reached Shanshan, King Guang of Shanshan received him with all courtesy and respect. Afterwards the king suddenly grew distant and negligent. Chao said to his officers: \"Do you not notice that Guang's courtesy has grown thin? This must be because envoys of the northern foe have come, and he is wavering and does not know which side to follow. A clear-sighted man perceives what has not yet sprouted, and how much more what is already plain.\" He then summoned a Hu attendant and tested him with a lie, saying: \"The Xiongnu envoys have been here several days. Where are they now?\" The attendant, terrified, confessed the whole matter. Chao had him shut away, assembled all thirty-six of his officers and men, and drank with them. When the wine had gone round he worked them into anger, saying: \"You and I are together in a land cut off from home, and we mean to establish great merit and win wealth and rank. Now the enemy's envoys have been here only a few days, and already King Guang's respect has failed. If Shanshan should seize us and hand us over to the Xiongnu, our bones will long be food for jackals and wolves. What is to be done?\" His officers all said: \"We stand now in a place of death and danger; in life and death we follow the major.\" Chao said: \"If you do not enter the tiger's den, you do not get the tiger's cub. The plan for today is simply to attack the enemy by fire in the night, so that they cannot tell how many we are. They will certainly be thrown into great terror, and we can wipe them out entirely. Once these foes are destroyed, Shanshan will lose its courage, and the work will be accomplished.\" The company said: \"This should be discussed with the aide.\" Chao answered angrily: \"Weal or woe is settled today. The aide is a common clerk; if he hears of this he will surely take fright and the plan will leak, and we shall die without even a name to our deaths. That is no way for a stout fellow.\" The company said: \"Good.\" In the first watch of the night he led his officers and men in a rush upon the enemy camp. It happened that a great wind was blowing. Chao ordered ten men to take drums and hide behind the enemy's huts, agreeing with them: \"When you see the fire blaze up, then beat the drums and shout with all your might.\" The rest, all armed with weapons and crossbows, lay in ambush on either side of the gate. Chao then set fire downwind, and drums and shouting arose before and behind. The enemy host, startled and thrown into confusion, was set upon; Chao killed three men with his own hands, and his officers and soldiers cut down the envoy and more than thirty of his followers, while the remaining hundred or so were all burned to death. The next day he returned and told Guo Xun. Xun was greatly alarmed, and then his expression changed. Chao understood what was in his mind, raised his hand, and said: \"Though you, sir, did not go, how should Ban Chao have the heart to take the whole credit for himself?\" Xun was then pleased. Chao thereupon summoned King Guang of Shanshan and showed him the head of the Xiongnu envoy; the whole state quaked with fear. Chao explained matters to them and soothed and comforted them, and the king sent a son as hostage. On his return Chao reported to Dou Gu, who was greatly pleased, laid Chao's merits before the throne in full, and asked that another envoy be chosen to go to the Western Regions. The emperor admired Chao's spirit and instructed Gu: \"When you have an officer like Ban Chao, why should you pass him over and choose another? Now appoint Chao army major, and let him complete the work already begun.\" Chao again received the mission. Gu wished to increase his troops, but Chao said: \"I ask only to take the thirty or so men who followed me from the first; that is enough. Should there be an unforeseen crisis, more men would only be a burden.\"\n"
"\n"
"At that time King Guangde of Khotan had just attacked and broken Shache and was extending his power over the southern road, while the Xiongnu had sent an envoy to watch over his kingdom. When Chao went west he came first to Khotan. Guangde's courtesy was very slight, and moreover the people of the country put their faith in shamans. A shaman said: \"Why is the god angry that you would turn toward Han? The Han envoy has a piebald horse; demand it at once and sacrifice it to me.\" Guangde accordingly sent a messenger to Chao to ask for the horse. Chao secretly learned what was behind it, replied that he granted the request, and ordered the shaman to come and take the horse himself. After a short while the shaman arrived. Chao at once cut off his head and sent it to Guangde, with a message of reproof. Guangde had long heard how Chao had destroyed the Xiongnu envoys at Shanshan; greatly frightened, he attacked and killed the Xiongnu envoy and submitted to Chao. Chao gave rich gifts to the king and all those below him, and so settled and pacified the country.\n"
"\n"
"At that time Jian, king of Kucha, had been installed by the Xiongnu. Trusting in the power of the foe, he held the northern road, attacked and broke Shule, killed its king, and set up a man of Kucha named Douti as king of Shule. The next spring Chao reached Shule by a side road. Ninety li from Pantuo city, where Douti lived, he sent his officer Tian Lü ahead to bring him over. He instructed Lü: \"Douti is not of the Shule stock, and the people of the state will certainly not obey him. If he does not submit at once, you may seize him.\" When Lü arrived, Douti saw that he was slight and weak and had not the least intention of submitting. Lü took advantage of his being unprepared, stepped forward, and bound him. Those about him, taken by surprise, all fled in terror. Lü galloped back to report to Chao, and Chao set out at once. He summoned all the generals and officers of Shule, laid before them the lawlessness of Kucha, and set up Zhong, the son of the former king's elder brother, as king; the people of the state were greatly pleased. Zhong and his officers all asked leave to kill Douti. Chao would not hear of it, wishing to display both authority and good faith, and released him and sent him away. From this time Shule was at enmity with Kucha.\n"
"\n"
"In the eighteenth year the emperor died. Yanqi, taking advantage of the great mourning in China, attacked and destroyed the Protector-General Chen Mu. Chao was left isolated and without support, while Kucha and Gumo repeatedly sent troops against Shule. Chao held Pantuo city, with Zhong as head and tail to him. His officers and men were few, but he held out for more than a year. When Emperor Zhang had just come to the throne, fearing that Chao, isolated and in peril, could not stand on his own, he sent down an edict recalling him. When Chao set out to return, the whole state of Shule was anxious and afraid. Its commandant Li Yan said: \"If the Han envoy abandons us, we shall surely be destroyed again by Kucha. I truly cannot bear to see the Han envoy go.\" Thereupon he cut his own throat with a knife. When Chao came back as far as Khotan, the king, the marquises, and all those below them wailed and wept, saying: \"We lean on the Han envoy as on father and mother; truly he must not go.\" They clung to the legs of Chao's horse, so that he could not set out. Chao feared that Khotan would after all not let him go east; he also wished to carry out his original purpose, and so turned back again to Shule. After Chao had left, two cities of Shule had gone over again to Kucha and joined forces with Yutou. Chao seized and beheaded the rebels, defeated Yutou, killed more than six hundred men, and Shule was again at peace.\n"
"\n"
"In the third year of Jianchu, Chao led ten thousand troops of Shule, Kangju, Khotan, and Jumi against the Stone City of Gumo, broke it, and took seven hundred heads. Wishing by this means to pacify the several states, he submitted a memorial requesting troops, saying: \"Your servant has observed that the late emperor wished to open the Western Regions; therefore he struck the Xiongnu in the north and sent envoys to the outer states in the west, and Shanshan and Khotan at once turned toward his transforming influence. Now Jumi, Shache, Shule, the Yuezhi, Wusun, and Kangju again wish to submit, and desire to join their strength to destroy Kucha and open a level road to Han. If Kucha is obtained, then of the Western Regions those that have not submitted will be but one in a hundred. Your servant has pondered it in his own heart. A petty officer of the ranks, I truly wish to follow Gu Ji and give my life in a land cut off from home, and perchance to follow Zhang Qian in casting away my body in the wilderness. Formerly Wei Jiang, a grandee of the feudal states, could still bring the Rong into harmony; how much more should I, who bear the majesty of great Han, be of some use, though it be no more than the single cut of a leaden knife? Those who debated the matter in former ages all said that the taking of the thirty-six states was called cutting off the Xiongnu's right arm. Now among the states of the Western Regions, from where the sun goes down, there is none that does not turn toward our transforming influence; great and small alike rejoice, and their offerings of tribute do not cease. Only Yanqi and Kucha alone have not submitted. Formerly I and thirty-six officers bore the envoy's commission into a land cut off from home and met with every hardship and peril. Since I have held Shule alone, five years have now passed, and I have come to know something of the temper and calculations of the Hu and the Yi. When I ask about the size of their walled towns, they all say, 「To lean on Han is the same as to lean on Heaven.」 Judging by this, the Onion Range can be opened; and if the Onion Range is opened, Kucha can be attacked. It is fitting now to appoint Baiba, the hostage prince of Kucha, king of that state, and to send him back with several hundred foot and horse, joining forces with the other states; within a year or so Kucha can be taken. To attack the barbarians with barbarians is the best of plans. I observe that the lands of Shache and Shule are rich and broad, their grass and pasture abundant, not to be compared with the country between Dunhuang and Shanshan; troops there need not drain China, and their food will be sufficient of itself. Moreover the two kings of Gumo and Wensu were installed by Kucha alone; since they are not of that stock, they resent and chafe against each other, and their situation must end in surrender or revolt. If these two states come over, Kucha will fall of itself. I pray that my memorial be handed down and the matter weighed in practice. If there is even a ten-thousandth part of hope, what regret would there be in death? I, Chao, am of small account, yet I have received special favour from the spirits, and I secretly hope that I may not yet fall stiff, but may with my own eyes see the Western Regions pacified, and Your Majesty raise the cup of ten thousand years, present the merit in the ancestral temple, and spread great joy throughout the world.\" When the memorial was submitted, the emperor knew that the work could be accomplished, and took counsel to grant him troops. Xu Gan of Pingling, who had long shared Chao's purpose, submitted a memorial asking to exert himself in Chao's support. In the fifth year Gan was therefore made acting major and led a thousand men, consisting of men under mitigated punishment and volunteer followers, to join Chao.\n"
"\n"
"Earlier, Shache, believing that Han troops would not come out, had submitted to Kucha, and Fan Chen, commandant of Shule, had also revolted again. It happened that Xu Gan arrived just then, and Chao with Gan attacked Fan Chen and utterly defeated him, taking more than a thousand heads and many captives. Having broken Fan Chen, Chao wished to advance against Kucha. Since the troops of Wusun were strong and their strength should be used, he submitted a statement: \"Wusun is a great state, with a hundred thousand bowmen. For that reason Emperor Wu gave them a princess in marriage, and in the time of Emperor Xuan he at last made use of them. Now an envoy may be sent to summon and soothe them, and to join forces with them.\" The emperor accepted this. In the eighth year Chao was appointed senior clerk in command of troops, and was granted for the occasion drums, trumpets, banners, and standards. Xu Gan was made army major. Separately, Li Yi, guard officer, was sent to escort the envoys of Wusun and to bestow brocade and silk on the greater and lesser Kunmi and those below them.\n"
"\n"
"When Li Yi first reached Khotan, it happened that Kucha was attacking Shule. In his fear he did not dare advance, and therefore submitted a memorial declaring that the work in the Western Regions could not be accomplished; he also slandered Chao bitterly, saying that he embraced a beloved wife, dandled a beloved son, took his ease in a foreign land, and had no heart turned back toward home. When Chao heard of it he sighed and said: \"I am no Zeng Shen, yet slander has reached me three times; I fear I shall be doubted in this age.\" He therefore put away his wife. The emperor knew Chao's loyalty and sharply reproved Li Yi, saying: \"Even if Chao embraces a beloved wife and dandles a beloved son, how could the thousand or more men who long for home all be of one mind with Chao?\" He ordered Yi to go to Chao and receive his direction. The edict to Chao read: \"If Yi can be employed abroad, then keep him with you as an aide.\" Chao at once sent Yi to escort the hostage prince of Wusun back to the capital. Xu Gan said to Chao: \"Yi slandered you to your face and wished to ruin the Western Regions; why do you not take advantage of the edict to keep him, and send another officer to escort the hostage prince?\" Chao said: \"How crude that saying is! Because Yi slandered me, I now send him away. If I search my own heart and find no fault, why should I care for men's talk? To keep him for my own satisfaction would not be the act of a loyal minister.\"\n"
"\n"
"The next year, acting major He Gong and three others were again sent with eight hundred troops to Chao, and Chao thereupon raised troops from Shule and Khotan to attack Shache. Shache secretly sent an envoy to Zhong, king of Shule, and tempted him with heavy profit; Zhong then revolted and went over to them, holding Wuji city in the west. Chao accordingly set up Cheng Da, the assistant of his office, as king of Shule, and raised all those who had not revolted to attack Zhong. The siege dragged on for half a year, and then Kangju sent picked troops to rescue him, so that Chao could not bring the city down. At that time the Yuezhi had newly made a marriage alliance with Kangju and were on close terms with them. Chao therefore sent envoys carrying much brocade and silk as a gift to the king of the Yuezhi, to have him make the matter clear to the king of Kangju; whereupon the king of Kangju withdrew his troops, seized Zhong, and took him back to his own country, and Wuji city then surrendered to Chao.\n"
"\n"
"Three years later, Zhong persuaded the king of Kangju to lend him troops and came back to occupy Sunzhong. In secret he plotted with Kucha and sent envoys to Chao with a feigned surrender. Chao knew his treachery in his heart, but outwardly pretended to consent. Zhong was overjoyed and came to Chao with light cavalry. Chao secretly ordered out his troops to wait for him and had provisions and music prepared. When the wine had gone round, he shouted to his officers to bind Zhong and behead him. He then attacked and broke his following, killing more than seven hundred men, and the southern road was from this time open.\n"
"\n"
"The next year Chao raised twenty-five thousand troops from the states of Khotan with the others and again attacked Shache, while the king of Kucha sent his general of the left to raise fifty thousand men from Wensu, Gumo, and Yutou to rescue it. Chao summoned his officers and the king of Khotan to council and said: \"Our troops are now too few to match theirs, and the best plan is for each of us to scatter and go his own way. Khotan shall go east from here, and I too will return west from here. Wait for the sound of the night drum and then set out.\" He secretly released the captives he had taken. When the king of Kucha heard of it he was greatly pleased and went in person with ten thousand cavalry to block Chao on the western border, while the king of Wensu led eight thousand cavalry to intercept Khotan on the eastern border. Chao, knowing that the two enemy forces had already come out, secretly summoned his divisions, ordered out his troops, and at cockcrow galloped to the Shache camp. The Hu were thrown into great confusion and fled, and he pursued and cut down more than five thousand, taking a great store of their horses, cattle, and goods. Shache then surrendered, and Kucha and the rest each withdrew and dispersed; from this time his might overawed the Western Regions.\n"
"\n"
"At first the Yuezhi had once aided Han in attacking Cheshi with success, and in this year they sent as tribute precious treasures, a fu-ba, and a lion, and asked for a Han princess in marriage. Chao refused and sent back their envoys, and for this reason they bore a grudge. In the second year of Yongyuan, the Yuezhi sent their deputy king Xie with seventy thousand troops against Chao. Chao's force was small, and all were greatly frightened. Chao explained to his soldiers: \"The Yuezhi troops are many, but they have come several thousand li across the Onion Range and have no transport of supplies. What is there to fear? We need only gather in the grain and hold firm; when they are hungry and at their wits' end they will surrender of themselves. The matter will be settled in no more than a few tens of days.\" Xie then advanced and attacked Chao, but could not take him, and plundered and got nothing. Chao judged that his provisions would soon be exhausted and that he must seek help from Kucha, and so sent several hundred troops to intercept him on the eastern border. Xie in fact sent cavalry carrying gold, silver, pearls, and jade to bribe Kucha. Chao's ambush fell upon them and killed them all, and he held up the head of the envoy for Xie to see. Xie was greatly alarmed and at once sent envoys to beg pardon and ask leave to return alive. Chao released and sent him away. The Yuezhi were greatly shaken by this and sent tribute every year.\n"
"\n"
"The next year Kucha, Gumo, and Wensu all surrendered, and Chao was made Protector-General and Xu Gan senior clerk. Baiba was appointed king of Kucha and Major Yao Guang was sent to escort him. Chao and Guang together forced Kucha to depose its king Youliduo and set up Baiba, and had Guang take Youliduo back to the capital. Chao resided at Qigan city in Kucha, while Xu Gan was stationed at Shule. In the Western Regions only Yanqi, Weixu, and Yuli, because the Protector-General had formerly been destroyed there, harboured divided loyalties; all the rest were settled.\n"
"\n"
"In the autumn of the sixth year, Chao raised the troops of Kucha, Shanshan, and six other states, seventy thousand in all, together with fourteen hundred officers, soldiers, and merchants, and marched against Yanqi. When his troops reached the border of Yuli he sent men to reason with Yanqi, Yuli, and Weixu, saying: \"The Protector-General has come to settle and pacify these three states. If you wish at once to mend your ways and turn to what is good, you should send your elders out to welcome him; the kings and marquises and all below them shall be rewarded, and when the business is done he will return at once. Now I bestow on the king five hundred bolts of coloured silk.\" King Guang of Yanqi sent his general of the left, Beijianzhi, with oxen and wine to welcome Chao. Chao interrogated Jianzhi, saying: \"You are a hostage son of the Xiongnu, yet you now hold the power of the state in your hands. The Protector-General has come in person, and your king has not welcomed him in due time; all of this is your crime.\" Some said to Chao that he might as well kill him. Chao said: \"That is beyond you. This man's authority is greater than the king's; if I kill him now before entering their state, I shall only make them suspicious, and they will set defences and hold the passes, and how then should I reach their city walls?\" So he gave him gifts and sent him back. Guang then came with his elders to welcome Chao at Yuli and presented precious things.\n"
"\n"
"The state of Yanqi had the defile of the Reed Bridge, and Guang broke the bridge down, not wishing to let the Han army enter the state. Chao went by another road and crossed by wading. On the last day of the seventh month he reached Yanqi. Twenty li from the city he encamped in a great marsh. Guang, taken by surprise, was greatly frightened and wished to drive all his people into the mountains for safety. Yuan Meng, marquis of the left of Yanqi, had formerly been a hostage at the capital. He secretly sent an envoy to tell Chao of the matter, and Chao at once had him beheaded, to show that he would not trust him. He then fixed a time for a great assembly of the kings of the several states, and let it be spread abroad that he would bestow lavish rewards. Thereupon King Guang of Yanqi, King Fan of Yuli, Beijianzhi, and thirty others came one after another to Chao. Seventeen men, including the chancellor Fujiu, fearing execution, all fled into the sea, and the king of Weixu also did not come. When they were seated, Chao angrily demanded of Guang: \"Why has the king of Weixu not come? On what account have Fujiu and the rest fled?\" He then shouted to his officers and men to seize Guang, Fan, and the others at the old city of Chen Mu and behead them, and sent their heads to the capital. He then let his troops plunder; more than five thousand heads were taken, fifteen thousand captives, and more than three hundred thousand head of horses, cattle, and sheep. Yuan Meng was set up in place of Guang as king of Yanqi. Chao remained in Yanqi half a year, soothing and comforting the people. Thereupon the more than fifty states of the Western Regions all sent hostages and became subjects of the interior.\n"
"\n"
"The next year an edict was sent down, saying: \"In former times the Xiongnu alone held the Western Regions and plundered Hexi, so that at the end of Yongping the city gates were closed by day. The late emperor, deeply grieved that the people of the frontier should be caught in the havoc of raiders, commanded his generals to strike the lands on the right; they broke White Mountain, came to Pulei, and took Cheshi, and the walled states were shaken and answered the call. Thus the Western Regions were opened and a Protector-General was established. But King Shun of Yanqi and his son Zhong alone plotted rebellion, trusting in their defiles, and destroyed the Protector-General and his officers and men as well. The late emperor, weighing the lives of the common people and dreading the raising of levies, therefore sent the army major Ban Chao to settle and pacify the land west of Khotan. Chao then crossed the Onion Range and went as far as the Suspended Crossing, coming and going for twenty-two years, and none failed to submit and follow him. He replaced their kings and brought peace to their people. He did not disturb China nor trouble her soldiers, yet he won the harmony of the distant Yi and united the hearts of peoples of different customs, and brought down Heaven's punishment, wiped out an old shame, and avenged the officers and men. The Methods of the Sima says: 「Rewards are not delayed beyond the month; the wish is that men may quickly see the profit of doing good.」 Let Chao be enfeoffed as Marquis of Dingyuan, with a fief of a thousand households.\"\n"
"\n"
"Chao himself, feeling that he had long been in a land cut off from home and that he was old and yearned for his native soil, in the twelfth year submitted a memorial, saying: \"Your servant has heard that when Tai Gong was enfeoffed in Qi, for five generations his descendants were buried in Zhou; that the fox dies with its head toward its hill, and the Dai horse leans into the wind. Now Zhou and Qi both lay within the thousand li of the central lands, and yet it was so; how much more should a petty servant, far away in a land cut off from home, be without the longing of the horse for the wind and of the fox for its hill? It is the custom of the Man and Yi to fear the strong and insult the old. Your servant Chao's teeth are gone, as those of a dog or horse; I am ever in fear that my years will fail and that I shall suddenly fall stiff, my orphaned soul cast away. Formerly Su Wu remained among the Xiongnu for nineteen years; now I have been fortunate enough to bear the staff of office and to wear gold and silver in guarding the Western Regions. If I should end my days in the garrison, I should truly have no regret, but I fear that later ages may name me as one lost in the Western Regions. I do not presume to hope to reach Jiuquan commandery, but I would only wish to enter the Jade Gate alive. I am old, sick, worn, and exhausted, and I risk death to speak these blind words. I respectfully send my son Yong with the tribute goods into the frontier. While I am still alive, let Yong see the central lands with his own eyes.\" And Chao's younger sister Zhao, wife of Cao Shou of the same commandery, also submitted a memorial begging for Chao, saying:\n"
"\n"
"\"Your handmaid's brother by the same mother, Chao, Protector-General of the Western Regions and Marquis of Dingyuan, has been fortunate enough to receive extraordinary rewards for small merit; his rank stands among the marquises of the highest order, and his position is that of two thousand shi. The grace of Heaven is without peer, and truly more than a petty servant should be permitted to receive. When Chao first went out, he was resolved to cast away his life, hoping to establish some small merit and so display his service. It happened that the upheaval of Chen Mu occurred and the roads were cut off. Chao, with his single person, turned this way and that in a land cut off from home, reasoning with the several states and using their troops, and in every attack and battle he was always first to mount the wall; his body has been wounded by metal, and he has never avoided death. Thanks to the favour of Your Majesty's spirits, he has been able to prolong his life in the desert, and to this day thirty years have accumulated. Flesh and bone have been parted in life and no longer know one another. Of the men of that time who followed him, all are dead. Chao is the eldest in years, and is now nearly seventy. He is aged, worn, and ill; his hair has no black left in it; his two hands are numb; his ears and eyes are no longer clear; he can walk only with a staff. Though he should wish to exhaust all his strength to requite the grace of Heaven, the close of the year presses upon him and the teeth of his dog and horse are gone. It is the nature of the Man and Yi to be perverse and to insult the old, and Chao may enter the ground at dawn or dusk; if for long no one is sent to replace him, I fear he will open a source of villainy and beget hearts of rebellion. And the ministers and grandees all harbour the thought of getting through the day, and none is willing to take thought for what is far off. If some sudden violence should occur, Chao's strength of breath cannot follow his will, and then above he will have damaged the merit of many generations of the state, and below he will have cast away the efforts of a loyal minister to the utmost — truly this is to be grieved over. Therefore Chao, from ten thousand li away, turns in sincerity and sets out his bitter straits himself, stretching out his neck and looking beyond, and for three years now he has not received your examination and record.\n"
"\n"
"Your handmaid has secretly heard that in ancient times men received arms at fifteen and returned them at sixty, and also that there was rest and release from office. Since Your Majesty governs the world by the highest filial piety and has won the joyful hearts of ten thousand states, not casting aside the servants of small states — and how much more one who has been permitted to hold the rank of marquis and earl — I therefore dare to risk death to beg compassion for Chao and to ask for the remainder of his years. If once he may return alive and see the palace courts again, it will be that the state will forever be free of the anxiety of distant toil, the Western Regions free of the worry of sudden crisis, and Chao may long enjoy the grace shown when King Wen buried the bones and when Zifang pitied the aged. The Songs say: 「The people also are wearied; grant them a little respite. Give favour to this central land, and so bring peace to the four quarters.」 Chao has sent me a letter bidding me farewell in life, fearing that we shall not meet again. Your handmaid is truly grieved that Chao should exhaust loyalty and filial piety in the desert in his strong years, and then, worn and old, be cast away to die in the wilderness; truly he is to be pitied. If he is not granted rescue and protection, and some day a change should come upon Chao, I would hope that the family of Chao might obtain the indulgence granted when Mother Zhao and Lady Wei made their earlier pleas. Your handmaid is foolish and stupid and does not know the great principles, and has offended against taboo.\"\n"
"\n"
"When the memorial was submitted, the emperor was moved by her words and recalled Chao.\n"
"\n"
"Chao was thirty-one years in the Western Regions. In the eighth month of the fourteenth year he reached Luoyang and was appointed colonel of the archers. Chao had long suffered from a disease of the chest and ribs, and after his arrival the illness grew worse. The emperor sent a Yellow Gate attendant to ask after his illness and bestowed medicine on him. In the ninth month of that year he died, at the age of seventy-one. The court pitied him; envoys were sent to offer sacrifice, and the gifts for the dead were very generous. His son Xiong succeeded to his title.\n"
"\n"
"At first, when Chao was recalled, Ren Shang, colonel of the Wu and Ji corps, was made Protector-General, and he took over from Chao. Shang said to Chao: \"You, my lord, have been in a foreign land for more than thirty years, and I, a small man, have presumptuously taken up your succession. My charge is heavy and my thought shallow; you should have something to teach me.\" Chao said: \"I am old and have lost my wits, while you, sir, have repeatedly held great office; how could Ban Chao come up to you? Yet if it must be, I would offer a foolish word. The officers and men beyond the frontier are not filial sons and obedient grandsons to begin with; they are all men who by their crimes have been sent to fill up the frontier garrisons. And the Man and Yi harbour the hearts of birds and beasts; they are hard to rear and easy to lose. Now your nature is strict and impetuous. Clear water has no big fish, and a government that pries into everything will not have the harmony of those below. You should be easy and simple, overlook small faults, and keep hold only of the main cords.\" After Chao had gone, Shang said privately to those close to him: \"I expected that Ban would have some extraordinary plan; what he has said today is quite ordinary.\" Within a few years of Shang's arrival the Western Regions revolted in disorder, and he was recalled for his crimes, just as Chao had warned.\n"
"\n"
"Chao had three sons. The eldest, Xiong, rose by successive steps to colonel of garrison cavalry. When the rebellious Qiang raided the three metropolitan districts, Xiong was ordered to lead the troops of five camps to station at Chang'an, and was appointed on the spot governor of the capital district. When Xiong died, his son Shi succeeded to the title, and married the Princess Yincheng, daughter of Xiao, prince of Qinghe. The princess was the aunt of Emperor Shun, proud, arrogant, and licentious; she kept a favourite in her curtained chamber, and summoned Shi to enter and made him lie beneath the bed. Shi's anger accumulated, and in the fifth year of Yongjian he drew a blade and killed the princess. The emperor was furious and had Shi cut in two at the waist, and all his brothers were executed in the marketplace. Chao's youngest son was Yong."

